#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "6dea6cd8-9392-5c72-bd4e-af7404c07d17" :question/math-academy-id "e-3457" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x+1=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sin{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sin x &= -1 \\\\\n\\sin x &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (-\\frac{1}{2}) =-\\frac{π}{6}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nis not in the range\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nTo get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply add $2 \\pi$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= -\\frac{π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{11π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/b170f4c1e6d9e1c8a09d09c59b5ba53c.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "532584f8-f656-5e25-a86c-6e272468dd6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3458" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^{\\circ} < x \\leq 180^{\\circ}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin x - \\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sin x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\sin x &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = 60^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $60^{\\circ}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/2db14a4844cbf6c495543c6905847053.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 180^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= 120^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = 60^\\circ, 120^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "da4b0fce-026c-50b0-ade0-3cb753027d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3460" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\sin{x} = 1$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (1) = 90^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $90^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ + n (360^\\circ)\n$$\n\nSince the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nis one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nis the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091932 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "eaee28f0-8458-59b2-a4d1-e86e3dcda3d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3511" :question/problem "Calculate, in radians to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\sin x = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{4}{5}) = 0.927295… \\approx 0.927\n$$\n\nSince $0.927$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.927\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.927\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.927\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/14ea617c25681a19eca6f44a19ff4aa8.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.927 \\\\\n&= 2.215\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.9, 2.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa8977ac-e0bd-5261-8984-7a0637b2fa3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265319} {:answer/id #uuid "10d94569-9f19-5933-92b1-c0077a8f02eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={59}^{∘},{121}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265320} {:answer/id #uuid "f3992956-1191-5178-be27-96a3227ea006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={33}^{∘},{147}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265321} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc103b4-a80a-51db-b98d-ee38391d5f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={41}^{∘},{139}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265322} {:answer/id #uuid "fd650180-c584-552e-983f-b1e86bd1d806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={49}^{∘},{131}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa8977ac-e0bd-5261-8984-7a0637b2fa3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265319} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d01cbc-623a-5367-b7ea-1056e8ee04b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265318}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b24174ad-9291-5093-8bdb-f1435a702636" :question/math-academy-id "q-120835" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=38.682{…}^{∘}≈{38.68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${38.68}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/12b17e3994be5e45af3aca185b22618dd0ec7288fba18a4131387783323247a0.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{38.68}^{∘} \\\\  & ={141.32}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f2f7862-ceb2-5d9e-98c4-913ba58db55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{122}^{∘},-{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265326} {:answer/id #uuid "04774c03-f698-556f-963c-f1c091a60392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{132}^{∘},-{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265327} {:answer/id #uuid "6b511b57-00ae-5915-af07-2742e94b915f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{112}^{∘},-{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265328} {:answer/id #uuid "b93f5ce3-db39-50ee-892f-c72ac540227e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{142}^{∘},-{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265329} {:answer/id #uuid "efaeb349-441c-5525-93ed-71d8c08768bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{122}^{∘},-{78}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2f7862-ceb2-5d9e-98c4-913ba58db55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{122}^{∘},-{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265326} :answer-field/id #uuid "65386fb3-866c-5fba-857f-c17ef7d735cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1854b83d-f26f-59a0-b8ab-54decd251347" :question/math-academy-id "q-17495" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{11}{13}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{11}{13})=-57.795\\,772{…}^{∘}≈-{57.796}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{57.796}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{57.796}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={57.796}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={57.796}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/f204faa6327d057dbea5de66f2c6ffca0a9982c299d1e4278dbc6514ddf016df.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{57.796}^{∘} \\\\  & ={237.796}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘}={237.796}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{122.204}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x=-{122}^{∘},-{58}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca5ee29f-38f8-500f-baa7-560bc2256f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265333} {:answer/id #uuid "991c0455-d30c-58f1-8874-052de9174b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991c0455-d30c-58f1-8874-052de9174b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265334} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d0a9c7-d834-5952-86e2-fddffe662513" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db78de7-b90a-517e-a43e-31ebd621d680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db78de7-b90a-517e-a43e-31ebd621d680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265336} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef4750c-7e14-5a4f-a30c-15e82e2884cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265335}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5395407a-550d-531d-be2e-5a1a73b82301" :question/math-academy-id "q-274615" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/b7b581dc39ba057033703c3549a721679d1d063267e13b65c73a9b9fcd66f1e6.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c46f2a38-ffd0-5e84-a14b-60b184402e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c46f2a38-ffd0-5e84-a14b-60b184402e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265339} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f8375f-b8b3-5341-a2da-1051cdb5b7b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6740a829-3fa0-536f-a658-fabe50839ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6740a829-3fa0-536f-a658-fabe50839ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265341} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd25aa3-1391-563d-be1c-84498d724cf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b293cb0-dbb6-5ad3-aa5f-1f351c2786fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-274616" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$  are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3})=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-0.729\\,7$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.7297.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/92b256db68d09a07e5072d12cb18470e2c165b6f3c8aa29c6953d9aebb3b024e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & =3.871\\,3-2\\pi ≈-2.4119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-2.4,\\,x≈-0.7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782b9fca-6fa3-5796-b1a9-015ed90a1631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "782b9fca-6fa3-5796-b1a9-015ed90a1631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265344} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ecfce4-fc0f-5d9f-a177-795e12cbc771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04221237-988a-510a-b6b7-e472f70baacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04221237-988a-510a-b6b7-e472f70baacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265346} :answer-field/id #uuid "c043081f-89f3-59aa-aa57-36bb165b0966" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265345}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5954b94-b8cd-5696-a81c-2e44aa692bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-328393" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arcsin\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/d50a4a9f62be8b8af4cc657a5b0ab1547288b844eaa05af1a57ed39b8afa0d6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90781274-5356-5a69-ae32-20250f80bf41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265349} {:answer/id #uuid "e79c6789-9094-559f-b5eb-44a4c969ad78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79c6789-9094-559f-b5eb-44a4c969ad78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265350} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5aad4b5-464a-5256-ac92-540930fdd30c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b94733-29fa-5c50-a3a1-da0cac98369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b94733-29fa-5c50-a3a1-da0cac98369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265352} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e3db6d-f7a1-57c9-a97b-038c1c460dda" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "892abfcf-428b-5897-9666-b647328728e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-330397" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8e25b80-a766-5b41-ac4e-ca268bd5bc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8e25b80-a766-5b41-ac4e-ca268bd5bc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265355} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f42c789-747e-5627-8f20-c8d3b9fd661f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98312467-7520-5292-991b-64a09c7f986e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98312467-7520-5292-991b-64a09c7f986e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d19ee50-c44d-5ec0-b6bf-4ede4bee3143" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265356}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e93742e6-32b2-5ea2-b7c6-d59a5b4d9938" :question/math-academy-id "q-330663" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=0.675\\,131…≈0.6751\n$$\n\nSince $0.675\\,1$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.6751.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6751.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6751.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/cc57a94c1f4f4fa53e35930c053b8f2e3f2be0fee0beb8d1584835a0f134b800.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.675\\,1 \\\\  & ≈2.4665\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈0.7,\\,x≈2.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1429e42c-562f-5917-b25e-ca5008b3353c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1429e42c-562f-5917-b25e-ca5008b3353c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265360} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1caffdd-019e-52f5-92f2-992cff113b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ff6a2ca-ec61-51fc-8cc5-ef00ae05a333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff6a2ca-ec61-51fc-8cc5-ef00ae05a333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265362} :answer-field/id #uuid "db237d7e-87c4-51d1-819e-1a834f2ce9dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ab73ac6-666a-53fb-9700-080fffc9262a" :question/math-academy-id "q-331941" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.8,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.8)=-0.927\\,295…≈-0.9273\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-0.9273$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-0.9273.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-0.9273+2\\pi ≈5.3559\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/942d3d727557ee280b93b212e856f4a696bcf06438944299fd6596dfb5b147f4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & ≈4.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are  \n\n$$\nx≈4.1,\\,x≈5.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6abb14c8-ee35-5239-84d7-329511240beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265365} {:answer/id #uuid "eccbad77-5cbf-5fdf-a4eb-40f1c2b91683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abb14c8-ee35-5239-84d7-329511240beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265365} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3e03da-efec-5b44-bdb4-a37c4c752b80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0282ece3-addf-5893-9644-2e7c00306e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "336" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0282ece3-addf-5893-9644-2e7c00306e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "336" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265368} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d3e73e-0ead-544c-9943-97d1f6479dac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265367}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf2df75b-484a-58d6-a8d4-ae002ab4cfbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-332214" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.4,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.4)=-23.578\\,178{…}^{∘}≈-{23.578}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{23.578}^{∘}$ is not in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-{23.578}^{∘}.$ To do this, we simply add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{23.578}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={336.422}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={23.578}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={23.578}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/c587e119531c3363816f72e9f2e1acdcc1aba87f278caa658342cbff144b0dad.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{23.578}^{∘} \\\\  & ={203.578}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{204}^{∘},\\,x≈{336}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15580266-990f-55dc-b202-bb14b00462f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15580266-990f-55dc-b202-bb14b00462f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265371} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17ccb40-9d6b-5335-92a2-1be20755c4e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265370} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06db14ed-4f85-53ff-a5eb-6d4e399ecab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06db14ed-4f85-53ff-a5eb-6d4e399ecab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265373} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a8ecd3a-d05a-5449-b932-9b5b53d4bb32" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265372}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec3e1fb9-d781-5f7e-a220-d10cd6e8bc01" :question/math-academy-id "q-336334" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3=0,$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.6)=36.869\\,897{…}^{∘}≈{36.870}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $36.870$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={36.870}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={36.870}^{∘}.$\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={36.870}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/38b2ec0588022556acd825c6b351d572d52034c6ec5cdeddbdff1dbf7baa11a2.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nX={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{36.870}^{∘}={143.130}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince this value is already inside the required domain, we keep it as our second solution.\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx≈{37}^{∘},\\,x≈{143}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5278254-d9f4-5a7d-af87-3aec813d39e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265376} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ad89c8-de5f-5d3b-9ea7-f90855532f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5278254-d9f4-5a7d-af87-3aec813d39e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265376} :answer-field/id #uuid "75f6a12e-3c43-5303-9ac6-14fa8ed000d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265375}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92c7a61a-1b24-5973-8dc6-9a50be918a9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336371" :question/problem "Solve the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Give your answer in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{90}^{∘}$ does not lie inside the required domain, we add one full period of ${360}^{∘}$ to find an equivalent angle in the domain:\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThus, our first solution in the required domain is ${x}_{1}={270}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={270}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aaefa1c-7b81-5b24-909d-d8be01abf5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265380} {:answer/id #uuid "bad0e88f-80b3-54da-9cba-48cd36e341d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad0e88f-80b3-54da-9cba-48cd36e341d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265381} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b027324-1561-50d1-8084-19ff62b1720c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265379}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc58faac-ba81-5140-b800-d51ae6619aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336379" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation  $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ Give your answer in radians.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ada4277c-692f-5571-8dd7-51ca9653374d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265384} {:answer/id #uuid "53c4bb85-fcfc-571b-bbc2-c491b3bddd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265385} {:answer/id #uuid "17b4ed42-a300-540d-bbe6-9c218749d561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265386} {:answer/id #uuid "7762dbc3-1344-5221-8056-60499bebcecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265387} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbf3c7e-1e2b-58a3-ab52-ed604d15928b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ada4277c-692f-5571-8dd7-51ca9653374d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265384} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac424a1d-e694-5a65-bafd-347332fa85e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf9f9645-9c31-5537-b299-ec1550429963" :question/math-academy-id "q-72773" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2b075e9-ecd6-512a-8794-c76962fa23a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{135.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265391} {:answer/id #uuid "789bd05f-eb7d-588c-aeb0-407986658648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265392} {:answer/id #uuid "d5faf294-d323-527a-a5bc-54e6da758784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{139.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265393} {:answer/id #uuid "8d63ba89-d1ac-51d5-a2c7-2691d079d5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45.9}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265394} {:answer/id #uuid "0abfb4ab-4bff-5f5d-b077-54b5d46a7749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={50.1}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789bd05f-eb7d-588c-aeb0-407986658648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265392} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f58714-9c66-5ecf-9acb-b5feb9024f48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265390}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc66b404-2b99-5ef1-898b-f30b9a1ab25e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72779" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=48.590\\,377{…}^{∘}≈48.590\\,{4}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $48.590\\,{4}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/4864e26ba05324041220dff99d1929b4c280b77456295d00d13d246279b4e628.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-48.590\\,{4}^{∘} \\\\  & =131.409\\,{6}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af43ed6-6ed6-5d2e-8bb0-a63019e4c4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265398} {:answer/id #uuid "6e406ad8-6993-5f37-b1b0-4b56ccc50379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265399} {:answer/id #uuid "00f3e839-c938-5d78-84d4-a99b51dc0078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265400} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5b71e2-dab5-5a17-8fc4-92cbf972fd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265401} {:answer/id #uuid "f34b7938-c0b1-5402-a6a8-3e82d975be90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b5b71e2-dab5-5a17-8fc4-92cbf972fd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265401} :answer-field/id #uuid "4184cc1c-6b9c-510d-847e-5d8d41baec28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265397}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c3f12b8-0685-5578-a04a-53dba75239e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8431" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{20{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "954460b8-3c52-5727-8186-b7b3d4272d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265405} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5144bc-ff27-59fe-87e7-6444aa9da30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265406} {:answer/id #uuid "af46b789-b51a-506d-bbb0-9072f5c70cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265407} {:answer/id #uuid "590b4250-b0ab-567a-aa66-08a67da4eeb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265408} {:answer/id #uuid "8602cc4d-dd75-5d1a-b6a3-2af6a77e8d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af46b789-b51a-506d-bbb0-9072f5c70cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265407} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e3f008-b480-54b3-9d1b-16add72a5de4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5dc2b789-e2f1-573b-a8c7-91ecf7b0de81" :question/math-academy-id "q-8435" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1594908-f00c-5217-be7d-a7a74d1f71c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265412} {:answer/id #uuid "381ed357-d22d-5fc8-bc14-b4a9d49c21a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265413} {:answer/id #uuid "aeffb562-9114-543a-bbe2-1ca914dd8550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265414} {:answer/id #uuid "61532bd5-d33e-5e7a-9cb2-1c9d54fd5ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265415} {:answer/id #uuid "01519aff-8466-512f-b32f-c9ab38441858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01519aff-8466-512f-b32f-c9ab38441858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265416} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a69bd0-ac4e-5632-b30b-bb335315fa7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265411}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "619d6fe2-1442-54ad-8625-6210eaafb0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8436" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/269d503189ab1027c3d8028542ef0c6a0e2f146aef0f7916691428866ce1b894.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00527b3-9595-5ea6-84bf-0ac63d49a44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091326} {:answer/id #uuid "d890a5c0-1b6b-51c7-bf4c-2723786159ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091327} {:answer/id #uuid "ee125ca2-c57f-5890-b66a-ff3aac9755b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091328} {:answer/id #uuid "22909b5a-6149-510a-9177-049e3a5fbb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091329} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2d624c-ff8f-5903-bfe2-80af73733105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d890a5c0-1b6b-51c7-bf4c-2723786159ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091327} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e8fc8c-6d96-54ad-91e7-77f0561da5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3724924f-e528-5763-adca-bd553bee798e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8576" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x + 2 = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f88483-fb58-5083-995b-e2c47fb0d899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265419} {:answer/id #uuid "693dc3f6-d8a0-57f3-ac6c-29672825f04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265420} {:answer/id #uuid "f18ae463-24cd-526a-9ab2-cf9162993d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265421} {:answer/id #uuid "08dd75db-52fe-5aae-a63d-2a9af77f01d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265422} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc1e288-aec1-5302-8752-5455123228c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfc1e288-aec1-5302-8752-5455123228c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265423} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d0296e-d835-500c-b67b-42fc5db69ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265418}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94f21909-0674-569b-9e74-b7afb787ec1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8577" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-0.167$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & ≈3.309-2\\pi ≈-2.974\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-0.167\\,4)\\cdot (-2.974\\,2)≈0.5,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911c3709-947b-5463-b26f-c374df42a188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091332} {:answer/id #uuid "9bafd6b7-3616-59a8-854c-cc5d09e9c241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091333} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bdab81-d509-53e0-a688-4e5ef7e77dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091334} {:answer/id #uuid "2612e1e0-bdfa-5fcb-8b65-7861996213bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091335} {:answer/id #uuid "7df5c43b-525c-50e1-b592-7c24b4bc33a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bafd6b7-3616-59a8-854c-cc5d09e9c241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091333} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b4c855-78a2-5ad7-bfa5-443665a14153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2682364-1658-50d1-928e-ea83479fc6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8578" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\sin x =-1$ for $- π < x \\le π$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its  minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}]}
